course: dictionary
lesson: 1755
title: Words for strong fears
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a feeling that is stronger than the danger in front of you. Claustrophobia is one of them. It means a strong fear of small, closed or crowded places, such as lifts, tunnels and cupboards.
explain: The sentence gives you clues: «Locking someone in a dark place "as a joke" is not a joke if they panic; claustrophobia is a fear of small, closed spaces.» The words panic and dark place show that this fear is real and serious. The sentence also tells you what the word means, so you do not need to guess.
explain: In everyday talk, claustrophobia can mean a feeling of being trapped or squeezed in, even when no one is really scared. You might have a feeling of claustrophobia on a packed bus. The adjective is claustrophobic, as in a claustrophobic tunnel: one that makes people feel trapped.
fact: Claustrophobia is a noun, a name for a feeling or fear.
fact: «Claustrophobia» means a strong fear of small, closed or crowded spaces.
fact: «Claustrophobic» is the adjective, used to describe a place or a feeling, such as «a claustrophobic lift».
fact: People who «suffer from claustrophobia» may feel their heart racing and want to get out quickly.
fact: A phobia is a fear that is stronger than the real danger.
exercise: «Locking someone in a dark place "as a joke" is not a joke if they panic.» What is claustrophobia? | A kind of joke | A love of bright, open fields | A type of cold weather | *A fear of small, closed spaces
exercise: Which word is the adjective form of claustrophobia? | cupboard | claustrophobia | *claustrophobic | tunnel
exercise: A person who «suffers from claustrophobia» might feel what in a lift? | Happy and calm | *Their heart racing and a wish to get out | A wish to stay there all day | Hungry and sleepy
test: Which example is a fear of small, closed spaces? | *Panic in a crowded lift | Swimming in the sea | Walking in a big park | Reading in a field
test: What word in the sentence tells you the fear is serious? | Sentence | Joke | *Panic | Funny
exam: Which sentence uses claustrophobic correctly? | Claustrophobic walked to the shop | She had a claustrophobic for lunch | *The tiny, crowded lift felt claustrophobic | The claustrophobic sang loudly
